{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>And Aaron lifted up, etc.</b> In Torat Kohanim, parashah 29, they said: the verse is inverted — it should have said ״וַיֵּרֶד מֵעֲשׂוֹת״ (and he came down from performing) etc., [and then] ״וַיִּשָּׂא אַהֲרֹן״ (and Aaron lifted up) etc., for during his descent he lifted his hands, etc. And one needs to know why the verse should speak in this manner and not in the straightforward manner. Perhaps it is because when Aaron saw that the Shekhinah had not descended, he thought that perhaps his atonement for the incident of the calf was still lacking, since through its intermediary he brought a curse upon the enemies of Israel — and therefore ״וַיִּשָּׂא וְגוֹ׳ וַיְבָרֵךְ״ (and he lifted up, etc., and blessed), etc., and with this he came down from performing the completion of the sin-offering, etc. Or perhaps it is that even the blessing of Israel, since it serves as atonement for Aaron's sin, is subsumed under the sin-offering, for it too may be classified under the name חַטָּאת (sin-offering). And its saying <b>וַיֵּרֶד</b> (and he came down) — perhaps it is in the language of descent, for he felt his own lowliness, because he saw that he had finished performing everything and the Shekhinah had not descended. And you will find that they said, of blessed memory, in Torat Kohanim, parashah 19, that Aaron cried out before Moses, who had told him to draw near to the altar, and he was brought to shame, etc.; and Moses went in with him and they sought mercy, and the Shekhinah descended."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:54:42.768196059Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}